People may not want their medical records shared with others. They might worry that it could change their health insurance or job. Or they might not want people to know about any health or mental problems that could embarrass them. Sharing medical data can also show other personal details. There are three main parts of medical privacy: information privacy (controlling personal info), physical privacy (keeping records safe from others), and emotional privacy (respecting a patient’s feelings, beliefs, and choices). Doctors and therapists in many places follow rules that protect the doctor‑patient relationship, keeping information secret. In some places, these rules are also backed by law. These practices protect patients’ dignity and help them share all needed information to get the right care.

