 They entered a garden surrounded by a low wall with no openings. On one side of the wall was a camel that used its long neck to reach the plants inside, where it ate a feast. It then turned and, mockingly, asked the pig—who had been standing at the bottom of the wall and had not even looked at the garden’s bounty—"Would you prefer to be tall or short?"

Later they came to a second garden, this one enclosed by a high wall and a gate at one end. The pig entered through the gate, ate a generous share of the vegetables, then left laughing at the poor camel, who was too tall to fit through the gate. The camel was left outside and the pig taunted, "Now, would you prefer to be tall or short?"

After some discussion, they decided that the camel should keep its hump and the pig its snout. They agreed that being tall is good when it matters, and being short is also valuable in other situations.

