Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
Most of the nitrogen on Earth is in the air as nitrogen gas, which plants and animals can’t use. In the early 1900s, scientists learned how to turn that gas into useful fertilizers. This process is called the Haber‑Bosch method. Nearly all nitrogen in fertilizers is made this way.

The Haber‑Bosch method is carried out in big factories around the world. It mixes nitrogen gas with hydrogen gas and forces the mixture to a very high pressure—about 200 times the pressure at sea level. That’s like the pressure you would feel if you were 2,000 meters (about 6,500 feet) below the ocean, or if you stacked over six Eiffel Towers on top of each other! After the gases are pressurized, they are heated to a very high temperature, around 450 °C (842 °F). Keeping the gases under such high pressure and temperature needs a lot of energy. In fact, the Haber‑Bosch process uses about 1–2 % of all the energy used worldwide each year.

