Model: Rewritten Excerpt:
The universe can be seen as a big family tree. The biggest pieces are galaxies. Galaxies group together into larger families called clusters, and these clusters join to form superclusters. These big clusters line up in long strands called filaments, which stretch across almost all of the universe, leaving huge empty spaces in between. This creates a web that covers everything we can see.

Galaxies come in many shapes. Some look irregular, some are smooth and round, and others are flat and disk-like. The shape of a galaxy depends on how it was made and how it has changed over time. When galaxies meet, they can merge to form a new, bigger galaxy.

Disk galaxies, like our own Milky Way, have thin, flat shapes and spiral arms that swirl out from the center. They also have a halo of stars around them. Most galaxies contain a very massive black hole at their center. If this black hole is pulling in a lot of gas, it can power a bright center called an active galactic nucleus. Some galaxies also have smaller companions called dwarf galaxies or dense groups of stars called globular clusters.

Inside a galaxy, the building blocks are stars. These stars form from clouds of gas that pull together because of gravity. In the first stages, these gas clouds break into smaller groups called nebulae, and stars are born in clusters from these clouds. The process happens in a step-by-step, layered way, just like the bigger structure of galaxies and the universe.

