 The experiment measured surface slope with about 600 tests, but it was too delicate to give useful results. This would affect all the formulas that used the measurement. The water surface was found to be roughly level on average, not curved like some writers said. We took 565 sets of vertical velocity data and put them into 46 groups. All 46 average curves were very flat and curved downwards, except near an uneven bank, and looked like parabolas. The data only gave rough values for the parameters. The highest velocity line was usually below the service level and dropped in the rectangular channel from the center out to about half depth near the banks. Its drop seemed unrelated to depth, slope, velocity, or wind. Likely the air, which always slows the surface, kept the maximum velocity low, while wind had no effect because it was short-lived. At any vertical line, the mid-depth velocity was higher than the mean, and the bed velocity was the lowest.

