Tool Call: compute_grade_level with args {'text': 'High‑speed cameras take pictures very quickly. They can take more than 1,000 photos in one second or can show more than 250 pictures each second. They are used to record fast moving things. The pictures are stored on a memory chip or a hard drive. Later, you can watch the pictures slowly to see what happened.\n\nOlder high‑speed cameras used film. Modern cameras are all electronic. They use either a CCD or a CMOS sensor. They usually record over 1,000 frames per second and store them in memory. Scientists study the slow‑motion playback to learn about quick events.\n\nHigh‑speed cameras can be grouped into four types:\n\n* Film cameras that record on film.\n* Video cameras that record on electronic memory.\n* Framing cameras that record several pictures at once on film or on a network of CCD sensors.\n* Streak cameras that record a long line of pictures on film or memory.'}
