 A high-speed camera is a device that can take pictures very quickly—more than 1,000 times per second. It records fast-moving things, like a ball or a bird in flight, onto a memory card or a special storage. After it records the video, you can watch it slowly to see details you would miss in real time.

Old high-speed cameras used film, but modern ones use electronic sensors. They often use a type of chip called a CCD or a CMOS sensor. These sensors can capture about 1,000 frames per second and then save the images in memory for later viewing.

There are different kinds of high-speed cameras:
1. Film cameras that shoot on actual film.
2. Video cameras that record directly to electronic memory.
3. Framing cameras that take pictures in multiple places on the same image.
4. Streak cameras that record a series of thin lines of images.

