 Mother had dark, shiny bronze skin and tiny ripples in her black hair. She had dark eyes and a kind face. She seemed very patient, but she also had a strong determination hidden in her gentle nature. Our family were small farmers on Egremont Plain, between Great Barrington and Sheffield in Massachusetts. The plots of land were too small to support large families, so we were always poor. I don't remember feeling cold or hungry, but I do remember that shoes, coal, and sometimes flour made my mother worried in winter, and a new suit was a big event. Around the time I was born, the family was moving from farming to being hired workers. Some members left for the West, others went to the city to work as cooks or barbers. Mother worked for several years in house service in Great Barrington. After a disappointing love affair with a cousin who moved to California, she met and married Alfred Du Bois and moved to town to live by the golden river where I was born.

