Neuro‑myths matter because they shape how people think and act: they can alter how we view ourselves and how we view others. Consider gender again. At ages 8–9, girls and boys perform similarly in math, yet girls—and their parents—often rate their math ability lower than boys do. This shows that beliefs (such as “girls are not as strong in math”) can truly influence how children see themselves, which can then affect their actual performance. In one study, college students took a math test. Men performed better than women when told the test usually shows gender differences; when told it was a gender‑fair test, women performed as well as men. This matters because, by the end of education, small differences can grow large: 94 % of mathematics professors in the UK are men. This example illustrates why we must be cautious about neuro‑myths—what we believe about our own brains and those of others can actually come true.

