 With regard to hearing, it is first noted that all children are completely deaf for some time after birth. Professor Preyer did not hear any sounds from his child until the middle of the fourth day. The child turned its head toward a sound in the eleventh week, and by the sixteenth week this reaction had become as quick and definite as a reflex. At eight months, or a year before the child began to speak, the infant could tell the difference between a tone and a noise, as shown by its pleasure when hearing piano music; after the first year the child also enjoyed striking the piano itself. In the twenty‑first month the child danced to music, and in the twenty‑fourth month it began to imitate songs. Other observers report that some children can sing pitches correctly, and even a melody, as early as nine months. One such child sang in its sleep at that age, and at nineteen months could keep time with its hands while singing an air.

