Each tiny dot on an LCD is built from a very thin layer of liquid crystals sandwiched between two metal plates and two special filters. The metal plates give the liquid crystals power, but they don't stop light.

Light moves in waves that have a direction, called polarization. A polarizing filter only lets light with a specific direction through. Think of it like a ruler that can only pass through a narrow slot if it lines up perfectly. The two filters in an LCD are set at right angles to each other, so the openings face different directions. If there were no liquid crystals between them, the light that passes the first filter would be blocked by the second one.

