 Joel sat up as tall as he could, squeezed between a big, fat man and his kind friend. He looked left and right, and his tears were gone. Then a pair of black horses and a jingling harness stopped before a small brown house. A doctor ran over the stepping stones in a lively way, and Joel had to run to keep up. They were there with Grandma Bascom walking around looking for herbs drying in the woodshed. Polly held David’s hand while he lay on the grain bags. In five minutes the new doctor examined everyone, and Davie sat up with his head on Polly’s shoulder. No bones were broken; the problem was just the fright from falling. Polly’s cheek looked bright again, and Grandma Bascom kept saying, “Praise the Lord—and who are you, anyway?” while bobbing her cap. The doctor laughed and didn’t tell her.

