 The dust-man takes away the dirt and trash from the streets. He works for a company that helps clean up the city. Every time he brings back a load of trash, the company splits it into six parts. First, the loose dust is sold to people who make bricks and to farmers who need good soil. Second, the small black pieces, called cinders, are sold to brick makers to use in their brick-making. Third, old clothes, bone fragments, and old metal scraps are sold to people who sell them. Fourth, old tin and iron containers are sold to people who make wooden houses. Fifth, broken bricks and shells from animals are sold to people who build roads. Sixth, old shoes are sold to people who make blue paint. Sometimes, the dust-man finds things that are worth more than this. He was even turned into a character in a famous book by Charles Dickens called "Our Mutual Friend."

