A group called the GNU Project made pieces of a new program called G.N.U. But it didn’t have all the parts needed to run a computer. In 1991, Linus Torvalds started working on a free program to replace MINIX. He did this while he was at the University of Helsinki. This became the Linux kernel.

Linus shared the Linux kernel with people who used MINIX. He first called the new program “Freax.”

