 Einstein wrote five important papers in one year, but he had loved physics from when he was a child. In the Einstein home, people talked about science at dinner. His dad, Hermann, and his uncle Jakob ran a German business that made motors, lamps, light bulbs, and phones. That was fancy technology at the time, like a modern Silicon Valley company. Albert liked science and new ideas because of that.

Sometimes his parents took Albert to parties. He didn't need a babysitter. Albert sat on the couch, listening to music and thinking about math while everyone else danced. He liked doing math on paper, like a game.

Albert could concentrate very well. His sister, Maja, remembers that even when there was a lot of noise, he could sit on the sofa, take a pen and paper, carefully put a small ink pot on the back of the couch, and focus on a problem so much that the noise helped him instead of bothering him.

Albert was very smart, but he didn't think he was special. He said, "I don't have any special talents. I am just very curious."

